item(517,'P000969','construction','The constrained geodesic equation on an ellipsoid',r'For \(\langle A^{-1}x,x\rangle=1\), the notes give \(x_{tt}=-\nu A^{-1}x\), \(y=x_t\), and \(\nu=\langle A^{-1}y,y\rangle/|A^{-1}x|^2\).',r'Let \(A\) be a positive definite symmetric real matrix. For initial data satisfying \(\langle A^{-1}x,x\rangle=1\) and \(\langle A^{-1}x,y\rangle=0\), the displayed equation is the affinely parametrized geodesic equation of the induced Euclidean metric on the ellipsoid. The multiplier follows by differentiating the constraint twice.','E',r'The topic and source are identified exactly by \citet{moser1980quadrics}, a contribution to the 1979 Chern Symposium published in 1980.',['moser1980quadrics'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='differential geometry and integrable systems')
item(517,'P000969','theorem','Isospectrality of Moser’s projected matrix',r'With \(P_y=I-y\otimes y/|y|^2\), the matrix \(L=P_y(A-x\otimes x)P_y\) has constant eigenvalues along the geodesic. A parenthesis attributes this to Jacobi, December 1838.',r'For a nonconstant geodesic in the preceding ellipsoid, \(y\ne0\), and the spectrum, with multiplicities, of this symmetric matrix is independent of time. The zero eigenvalue belonging to \(y\) is included; the remaining spectral data encode the classical geodesic integrals.','E',r'The matrix formulation is the Moser presentation \citep[pp.~147--188]{moser1980quadrics}; Jacobi is the antecedent for integration of the ellipsoid geodesic problem. The notebook alone does not establish that Jacobi used this matrix or the precise December date.',['moser1980quadrics'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='Positive definiteness, nonzero velocity, and the distinction between Jacobi’s integrability and Moser’s matrix formulation are explicit qualifications.',area='differential geometry and integrable systems')

item(518,'P000969','construction','Elliptic rank-two operators in Tyurin coordinates',r'The notes give \(L_6^2=L_4^3+g_1L+g_0\), and \(L_4=(D^2-u)^2+2c_x(\wp(\gamma_2)-\wp(\gamma_1))D+[c_x(\wp(\gamma_2)-\wp(\gamma_1))]_x-\wp(\gamma_1)-\wp(\gamma_2)\), where \(\gamma_1=\gamma_0+c\), \(\gamma_2=\gamma_0-c\).',r'The Krichever--Novikov construction associates a commuting pair of orders four and six and generic common-eigenspace dimension two to elliptic spectral data and a locally regular Tyurin deformation. The bare \(L\) in the cubic relation means \(L_4\). Elliptic discriminant nonvanishing and avoidance of poles and of \(c_x=0\) are needed for the displayed local formula.','E',r'The construction predates this talk; \citet[Thms.~3.2--3.3]{mironov2012higher} reproduces the Krichever--Novikov and Dixmier formulas with explicit conventions. The notebook uses \(D^2-u\) where that account uses \(D^2+u\), so the potential formula must be compared with its sign convention.',['mironov2012higher'],match='partial',reconstruction='The commuting relation and nonsingularity assumptions are made explicit. The notebook’s unreconciled potential normalization is not asserted to be a verified transcription of the published formula.',confidence='medium')
item(518,'P000969-P000970','method','Tyurin deformation equations and the potential formula',r'The note records \(\gamma_{1,x}=1/(\alpha_1-\alpha_2)=-\gamma_{2,x}\), \(\alpha_{1,x}=\alpha_1^2-u-\varphi\), \(\alpha_{2,x}=\alpha_2^2-u+\varphi\), and \(u=1/(4c_x^2)-c_{xx}^2/(4c_x^2)-2\varphi c_{xx}-c_x^2(\varphi_c-\varphi^2)+c_{xxx}/(2c_x)\). Here \(\varphi=\zeta(\gamma_2-\gamma_1)+\zeta(\gamma_1)-\zeta(\gamma_2)\), \(\zeta_x=-\wp\), and \(\zeta\) is odd.',r'The first pair of equations forces \(\gamma_1+\gamma_2\) to be constant. These are local deformation formulas for the Tyurin data of the elliptic rank-two construction, valid away from coincident Tyurin points and singular denominators. The \(u\)-formula and the signs in the Riccati equations require a consistent choice of potential and elliptic normalization; the excerpt does not fix all of them.','E',r'Compare \citet[§3, Thm.~3.2]{mironov2012higher}. This is a substantive identification of the method, not a certification that every sign in the source formula is correct.',['mironov2012higher'],correct='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='medium',status='As of November 2026, the Tyurin deformation method and elliptic construction are established; the exact notebook potential normalization remains unresolved in this companion.')
item(518,'P000970','theorem','Grinevich’s rationality criterion and the Dixmier specialization',r'For rational \(q(x)\), the integral \(c(x)=\int_{q(x)}^\infty dt/\sqrt{t^3+g_1t+g_0}\) gives rational coefficients. The next line writes \(\gamma_0=0\Rightarrow q(x)=x\) and calls this the Dixmier case.',r'In the regular elliptic Krichever--Novikov family, rational coefficients are obtained by choosing the elliptic Abel parameter as the integral from a rational function \(q(x)\) to infinity; the conventional Weierstrass model uses \(4t^3+g_2t+g_3\). The centre \(\gamma_0\) is constant. The joint choices \(\gamma_0=0\) and \(q(x)=x\) yield the Dixmier specialization in the corresponding normalization; the first choice does not imply the second.','A',r'\citet{grinevich1982rational} was received 24 November 1980 and published in 1982. The criterion is stated explicitly as \citet[Thm.~3.4]{mironov2012higher}; the latter supports the mathematical identification while the original publisher record verifies chronology.',['grinevich1982rational','mironov2012higher'],correct='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The arrow is changed to a joint specialization, and the harmless cubic scaling is separated from the substantive rationality criterion.')
correction('P000969','Grinevich cubic relation',r'L6^2=L4^3+g1 L+g0',r'L6^2=L4^3+g1 L4+g0','The unscripted L is literal in the autograph; the displayed orders and elliptic spectral relation identify L4.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000970','Dixmier specialization',r'gamma0=0 => q(x)=x',r'gamma0=0 and q(x)=x','The arbitrary rational q is a separate choice; Mironov 2012 Thm3.4 reproduces the rationality criterion and identifies the joint specialization.')

item(519,'P000970','construction','Kirillov’s formal pseudodifferential formula for the Dixmier pair',r'With \(L=D^2+x^3\), \(A=L+xL^{-1}\), the notes give \(L_4=(A^2)_+\), \(L_6=(A^3)_+\), then \(L_4=L^2+2x\), \(L_6=L^3+3x^2L+3Lx^2\).',r'Here the positive part means the differential part of a formal pseudodifferential operator. The correct pair is \(L_4=L^2+2x\), \(L_6=L^3+\tfrac32(xL+Lx)=L^3+3xL+3D\). It satisfies \([L_4,L_6]=0\) and \(L_6^2=L_4^3\). Replacing \(L\) by \(D^2+x^3+a\) gives the nonsingular elliptic Dixmier cubic for nonzero suitable \(a\).','E',r'The construction is due to \citet{dixmier1968weyl}. The recorded sixth-order alternative is false; this chapter checks the corrected identity directly. The parameter-free source specialization has a singular cubic, so its normalization has genus zero, despite the surrounding elliptic discussion.',['dixmier1968weyl'],correct='false as written',match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',development='developed in this chapter')
item(519,'P000970','question','Rational differential operators for a prescribed genus-two equation',r'For a genus-two curve \(P(x,y)=0\), is there a pair of operators with rational coefficients satisfying \(P(L_1,L_2)=0\)?',r'For an irreducible affine equation \(P(z,w)=0\) whose smooth projective model has genus two, determine when there are commuting ordinary differential operators in \(\mathbb C(x)[D]\) for which the homomorphism \(\mathbb C[z,w]/(P)\to\mathbb C(x)[D]\), \(z\mapsto L_1,w\mapsto L_2\), is injective. Commutativity and injectivity are necessary reconstructions: the bare equation admits irrelevant constant solutions. A realization after changing affine coordinates or only at the function-field level is a different question.','C',r'\citet[Thm.~3]{mironov2011selfadjoint}, first circulated in 2011 and published in 2014, gives rank-two polynomial examples of every genus. \citet[Thm.~1]{davletshinamironov2016orbits}, circulated in 2016 and published in 2017, realizes generic genus-two quintics and studies infinitely many Weyl-automorphism orbits. Neither result states realization of every affine presentation. This chapter supplies a verified genus-two family and an affine-coordinate obstruction.',['mironov2011selfadjoint','davletshinamironov2016orbits'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1980-40',worth='Worth developing as a precise classification of realizable affine coordinate rings: generic existence and the explicit unit obstruction leave a substantive distinction between coordinates, spectral curves, and function fields.',development='developed in this chapter',status='As of November 2026, generic genus-two realizations and explicit families are established. The unrestricted claim for every affine presentation is false: the coordinate ring of z w^2-z^4-1=0 has a nonconstant unit, obstructing an injective realization in ordinary differential operators. A complete classification of realizable presentations is not supplied by the checked literature.',reconstruction='The source asks rather than asserts universal realization. Commutativity, faithfulness, the base field, and the coordinate-versus-birational distinction are explicit reconstructions.')
item(519,'P000970','conjecture','Curve function fields in the Weyl division ring',r'Gelfand proposes that any curve embeds in the division ring of fractions of the Weyl algebra. A marginal gloss specifies the field of rational functions on the curve, and an inserted word changes a possible theorem heading to conjecture.',r'For every smooth projective integral curve \(C/\mathbb C\), does there exist a unital \(\mathbb C\)-embedding \(\mathbb C(C)\hookrightarrow\operatorname{Frac}(A_1(\mathbb C))\), where \(A_1=\mathbb C\langle x,D\rangle/(Dx-xD-1)\)? Singular integral curves give the same question after normalization.','C',r'The Dixmier example gives genus one; \citet[Thm.~3]{mironov2011selfadjoint} supplies some hyperelliptic curves of every genus, and \citet[Thm.~1]{davletshinamironov2016orbits} supplies generic genus-two cases. These are exact positive subcases, not a proof for every curve. The chapter proves the field embedding for its displayed family and separates it from ordinary-operator affine-coordinate realizations. The earlier 1977-434.6 is a thematic precursor on commutative subalgebras, not an exact recurrence with a reusable problem ID.',['dixmier1968weyl','mironov2011selfadjoint','davletshinamironov2016orbits'],correct='not determinable',match='partial',problem='GP-1980-41',worth='Worth developing because the division ring allows inverses and negative differential orders, and the universal birational question is strictly broader than the known explicit spectral families.',development='developed in this chapter',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',status='As of November 2026, the checked primary literature establishes the indicated positive subcases; no source proving or disproving the full universal curve-function-field conjecture was located. This is a documented search limitation, not a claim that no such theorem exists.',confidence='medium')
item(519,'P000970','claim','The remark excluding coprime differential orders',r'The sentence following the genus-two question says that there are no coprime orders.',r'A precise verified polynomial-coefficient statement is: over a characteristic-zero field, if the centralizer of a nonconstant element of the first Weyl algebra has differential-order rank one, it embeds in a polynomial ring in one variable and has genus zero. Thus polynomial commuting operators of coprime positive orders cannot faithfully generate a positive-genus spectral field. The sentence in context concerns rational coefficients, for which this cited theorem alone is insufficient.','B',r'\citet[Thm.~1.1]{makarlimanov2021centralizers}, received January and published May 2021, proves the polynomial centralizer statement. Rank is the gcd of orders of all nonconstant elements of the commutative centralizer; it is not generally inferred from an arbitrary displayed pair.',['makarlimanov2021centralizers'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',confidence='medium',status='As of November 2026, the polynomial-coefficient version is established. The unrestricted rational-coefficient wording in this notebook is not certified by the source checked here.')
item(519,'P000970','problem','Existence of the Weyl skew field of fractions',r'The algebra generated by \(q,p\) with \(qp-pq=1\) embeds in a division ring, offered as an exercise.',r'The first Weyl algebra over a characteristic-zero field is a noetherian domain and satisfies the left and right Ore conditions for its nonzero elements. Its classical ring of fractions is consequently a division ring containing it injectively.','E',r'This is standard background to \citet{dixmier1968weyl}; \citet[§2]{makarlimanov2021centralizers} explicitly recalls the Ore embedding. The order of the generators only determines the sign convention for the defining commutator.',['dixmier1968weyl','makarlimanov2021centralizers'],correct='correct',match='exact')
