correction('P000970','speaker surname','Berlinskii / Brelinskii / Berlinski (?)','Jean-Luc Brylinski','The Kashiwara collaboration and the geometric Verma-module topic identify Brylinski, whose 1981 primary exposition is exact bibliographic evidence.','both','provenance')

item(520,'P000970','definition','Verma modules',r'For a complex semisimple \(\mathfrak g\), a Borel \(\mathfrak b\), and a character \(\lambda:\mathfrak b\to\mathbb C\), the notes introduce \(M_\lambda\).',r'The Verma module in the stated induction convention is \(M_\lambda=U(\mathfrak g)\otimes_{U(\mathfrak b)}\mathbb C_\lambda\), with the nilpotent radical of \(\mathfrak b\) acting trivially on \(\mathbb C_\lambda\). Shifts by \(\rho\), opposite Borels, and dual modules belong to alternative conventions and must be specified in a geometric realization.','E',r'The definition is established representation theory. \citet[§1]{brylinski1981differential}, presented at Toruń in August--September 1980 and published in 1981, gives the relevant geometric setting.',['brylinski1981differential'],correct='correct',match='exact',area='representation theory')
item(520,'P000970','construction','Flag-variety and projective-line realizations',r'The module is represented by geometric objects on the flag space. For \(SL(2)\), the sketch labels holomorphic functions on \(\mathbb P^1\setminus\{\infty\}\) meromorphic near infinity, and germs of meromorphic functions near infinity.',r'Flag-variety realizations use twisted differential operators and appropriate standard, costandard, or local-cohomology modules. For \(SL_2\), global functions on the affine line that are meromorphic at infinity are polynomials; local principal parts arise from a quotient of meromorphic germs by holomorphic germs. A weight-dependent action and the relevant duality convention are necessary to identify either space with a specified Verma module.','A',r'\citet[§§1--2]{brylinski1981differential} supplies the geometric framework. The note’s mention of the Kashiwara collaboration identifies the speaker; it does not state the Kazhdan--Lusztig theorem anew, so GP-1979-41 is not assigned to this abbreviated construction.',['brylinski1981differential'],match='partial',reconstruction='The twist, the principal-parts quotient, and the warning about dual Verma modules supply data missing from the diagram.',confidence='medium')

item(521,'P000971','definition','Kodaira dimension and irregularity',r'Viehweg defines \(\kappa\) using the graded algebra \(\bigoplus_{m\ge0}H^0(X,\Omega_X^{n\otimes m})\) and \(q=\dim H^0(X,\Omega_X^1)\).',r'For a smooth connected projective complex \(n\)-fold, let \(R(X,K_X)=\bigoplus_{m\ge0}H^0(X,K_X^{\otimes m})\). If every positive-degree piece vanishes, \(\kappa(X)=-\infty\); otherwise \(\kappa(X)=\operatorname{trdeg}_{\mathbb C}R(X,K_X)-1\). Irregularity is \(q(X)=h^0(X,\Omega_X^1)=h^1(X,\mathcal O_X)\).','E',r'These conventions are given at the start of \citet[pp.~111--112]{esnault1981classification}, Bourbaki exposé 568, February 1981, explicitly named by the lecture.',['esnault1981classification'],match='exact',reconstruction='Smoothness, projectivity, the zero-ring exception, and transcendence degree rather than an unspecified degree are supplied.')
item(521,'P000971','theorem','Maximal irregularity at Kodaira dimension zero',r'The table relates \(\kappa=0\), \(q=n\), and being birationally equivalent to an abelian variety; the direction of an arrow is unclear.',r'If \(X\) is smooth projective over \(\mathbb C\), \(\kappa(X)=0\), and \(q(X)=\dim X\), then its Albanese map is birational onto an abelian variety. Conversely a variety birational to an abelian variety has these invariants.','E',r'\citet[Thm.~1 and the adjacent table]{esnault1981classification} reports the Kawamata result before the seminar date. This is an exact subject match, although the notebook arrow is not silently read as an equivalence.',['esnault1981classification'],match='exact',reconstruction='The complete equivalence is the modern reconstruction; the manuscript arrow itself remains a palaeographic ambiguity.')
item(521,'P000971','theorem','Irregular threefolds of Kodaira dimension zero',r'For \(n=3\), \(\kappa=0\), and \(q=2\) or \(1\), the notes say there is an étale fibration and the fibre has \(\kappa=0\).',r'For the smooth projective complex threefolds in the cited classification, the Albanese description becomes a product after a suitable finite étale base change, up to the birational modifications allowed in the theorem. The general fibre has dimension \(3-q\) and Kodaira dimension zero. Calling the map itself étale is impossible when its fibres have positive dimension.','E',r'\citet[Thm.~2 and table, pp.~112--114]{esnault1981classification} is the explicitly identified source. The note compresses birational and finite-cover qualifications into the phrase about étaleness.',['esnault1981classification'],match='partial',reconstruction='Étale refers to the base change and local trivialization, not to a positive-dimensional-fibre morphism.')
item(521,'P000971','question','The irregularity-zero entry in the classification table',r'The line \(\kappa=0,q=0\) ends in a question mark.',r'The source does not specify whether it asks for a birational classification, a list of deformation types, the geometry of the Albanese map, or another invariant of threefolds with \(\kappa=q=0\). These are inequivalent problems.','F',r'The contemporary limitations and cases are surveyed by \citet[§1]{esnault1981classification}. That report identifies the context but cannot turn the isolated mark into one precise conjecture.',['esnault1981classification'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, the mark remains an incomplete classification entry in this record; no new globally indexed open problem is inferred.',confidence='high')
item(521,'P000971','remark','The negative-infinite Kodaira-dimension line',r'The \(\kappa=-\infty\) line records only an indefinite description.',r'No definite classification assertion, dimension restriction beyond the preceding discussion, or birational conclusion can be recovered from this fragment.','F',r'The surrounding report is \citet{esnault1981classification}; its results cannot be substituted for words that are absent in the notebook.',['esnault1981classification'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, this fragment has no determinate mathematical claim to certify.',confidence='high')
correction('P000971','definition of Kodaira dimension','degree of the canonical graded algebra minus one','transcendence degree minus one, with the no-positive-section case assigned minus infinity','The source abbreviation is imprecise; Esnault 1981 p111 supplies the exact definition.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000971','positive-dimensional Albanese fibres','étale fibration','fibration trivialized by finite étale base change, with the theorem’s birational qualifications','An étale map has relative dimension zero; Esnault 1981 Thm2 is the explicitly named source.','both','faithful_error')

item(522,'P000971-P000972','method','Waldhausen’s K-theory programme for diffeomorphism spaces',r'Kassel relates the homotopy type of a diffeomorphism group to the algebraic K-theory of a loop-space ring, invoking Waldhausen and homotopy rings.',r'Algebraic K-theory of spaces, \(A(X)\), is a homotopy-theoretic construction involving retractive spaces over \(X\), or equivalently appropriate modules over the suspension ring spectrum of \(\Omega X\). Its relation to stable pseudoisotopy and stable smooth h-cobordisms is not an identity between an arbitrary diffeomorphism group and the ordinary K-theory of \(\mathbb Z[\pi_1X]\).','E',r'\citet[pp.~35--60]{waldhausen1978topological} appeared in the proceedings of the 1976 Stanford symposium, published 1978. \citet[Introduction and §1]{kassel1983stabilisation} develops the homotopy-ring and stabilization context, received after this lecture and published in 1983. No exact diffeomorphism-space equivalence is stated in this notebook.',['waldhausen1978topological','kassel1983stabilisation'],match='partial',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The distinction between A-theory and ordinary group-ring K-theory is explicit; no unstated stable-range theorem is supplied.',area='algebraic topology and K-theory')
item(522,'P000972','construction','Quillen’s plus construction and homotopy rings',r'The notes compare Quillen’s plus construction for rings with a homotopy-ring construction and mention maps between the corresponding classifying spaces.',r'For a unital discrete ring \(R\), the positive K-groups are \(K_i(R)=\pi_i(BGL(R)^+)\), \(i\ge1\), with the plus construction killing the stable elementary subgroup. Passing to a ring structure defined only up to homotopy requires coherent multiplication and a compatible homotopical K-theory construction; a bare nonassociative operation up to an unspecified homotopy does not suffice.','E',r'The ordinary construction is Quillen’s earlier theory; \citet[§1]{kassel1983stabilisation} provides the relevant homotopy-theoretic refinement. Its 1983 publication is a partial identification of the discussion, not a proof that every diagram in the shorthand is the same theorem.',['kassel1983stabilisation'],match='partial')
item(522,'P000972','claim','Soulé’s zeta-value bounds for the orders of K-groups',r'Under a separate Soulé heading, for even \(i\ge2\) the notes bound \(|K_{2i-2}(\mathbb Z)|\) and \(|K_{2i-1}(\mathbb Z)|\) below by the numerator and denominator of \(\zeta(1-i)\). They cite Inventiones, February 1980, and a method involving topoi.',r'Write \(|\zeta(1-i)|=a_i/b_i\) in lowest terms, \(a_i,b_i>0\), with even \(i\ge2\). The modern finite-order formulas imply \(|K_{2i-2}(\mathbb Z)|\ge a_i\) and \(|K_{2i-1}(\mathbb Z)|\ge b_i\). Absolute numerator is necessary when the zeta value is negative. Étale Chern classes and arithmetic cohomology are part of the source context; the cryptic date does not identify an exact theorem by itself.','B',r'Soulé’s arithmetic paper was published in October 1979, not February 1980 \citep{soule1979k}. His 1978 thesis gives related lower bounds with prime restrictions. Complete formulas, including the factors at 2, are recorded in \citet[Thm.~95 and Table~5.1]{weibel2005rings}. The cited later formulas verify the inequalities but do not establish that their full strength was proved in the unidentified February reference.',['soule1979k','soule1978thesis','weibel2005rings'],match='partial',correct='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='C. Soulé',reconstruction='The sign convention for numerator and the separation between present validity and historical attribution are supplied.',status='As of November 2026, these numerical lower bounds follow from established arithmetic K-theory formulas. Exact attribution of the full displayed bounds to the manuscript’s February 1980 reference remains unresolved.',confidence='medium')
correction('P000972','Soulé bibliographic shorthand','Inventiones, February 1980','Unresolved shorthand; the identifiable arithmetic K-theory paper is Inventiones55 (1979),251–295','Soulé’s publication list and the publisher’s October1979 record were opened; no exact February1980 paper matching the line was identified.','both','provenance')
correction('P000972','numerator of zeta value','numerator of zeta(1-i)','absolute numerator, if the inequality is intended as a positive lower bound','For example zeta(-1)=-1/12; the group order is nonnegative.','both','ambiguity')
