item(523,'P000973','method','The collisionless self-consistent-field model',r'A large collection of charged particles creates a potential determined by its density; direct binary collisions are neglected.',r'A representative electrostatic formulation is \(f_t+v\cdot\nabla_xf-\nabla_x\phi\cdot\nabla_vf=0\), with a Poisson equation relating \(\phi\) to \(\rho(x)=\int f(x,v)\,dv\), including a background and boundary conditions when appropriate. This is a collisionless mean-field model; the notebook does not state a scaling limit from a finite particle system.','E',r'The model goes back to Vlasov’s 1938 work, reprinted in \citet{vlasov1968vibrational}. The opened publisher page verifies that identification and the topic, not a modern derivation theorem.',['vlasov1968vibrational'],correct='correct',match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='The displayed PDE is supplied to make the modelling description precise; electrostatic force and boundary conventions are not fixed by the notes.',area='kinetic theory')
item(524,'P000973','theorem','Incompressible Euler flow as geodesic motion',r'The quadratic form \(\frac12\int|v|^2\) on divergence-free vector fields is extended by left translations to volume-preserving diffeomorphisms; its geodesics are ideal-fluid flows.',r'For a compact oriented Riemannian manifold without boundary, or with the usual impermeable boundary condition, equip volume-preserving diffeomorphisms with the right-invariant weak \(L^2\) metric in the usual material-to-spatial convention. Its smooth geodesics have Eulerian velocity satisfying \(u_t+\nabla_u u=-\nabla p\), \(\operatorname{div}u=0\). An inverse-map convention interchanges left and right.','E',r'\citet[§§1--3]{arnold1966geometrie} supplies the formulation cited explicitly in the notes. The translation side must be reconciled with the velocity convention.',['arnold1966geometrie'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='The manifold, boundary condition, smooth lifespan, and right-translation convention are supplied.',area='geometric hydrodynamics')
item(524,'P000973-P000974','method','Hamiltonian reduction and coadjoint-orbit symplectic forms',r'The invariant kinetic energy on \(T^*G\) projects to \(\mathfrak g^*\), whose coadjoint orbits carry the Kirillov form.',r'For a finite-dimensional Lie group, an invariant Hamiltonian on its cotangent bundle reduces under the lifted translation action to a Lie--Poisson Hamiltonian on \(\mathfrak g^*\). Its symplectic leaves are coadjoint orbits with the Kirillov--Kostant--Souriau form. Infinite-dimensional fluid versions require a chosen regular dual, functional setting, and smoothness domain.','E',r'The Euler-group reduction is part of \citet{arnold1966geometrie}. The Vlasov application is developed by \citet{marsdenweinstein1982hamiltonian}, received August 1981 and published March 1982.',['arnold1966geometrie','marsdenweinstein1982hamiltonian'],match='partial',reconstruction='The note’s phrase that the Kirillov form descends is interpreted through reduction; it is not a globally nondegenerate form on all of the dual.',area='symplectic geometry')
item(524,'P000973','construction','The Vlasov Lie–Poisson bracket',r'The group of canonical transformations is said to produce the Vlasov equation, with the parenthetical phrase cold plasma.',r'On a particle phase space with canonical Poisson bracket, smooth density functionals have the Lie--Poisson bracket \(\{F,G\}(f)=\int f\{\delta F/\delta f,\delta G/\delta f\}_{x,v}\,dx\,dv\), with a consistent overall sign convention. The kinetic-plus-self-field Hamiltonian yields Vlasov evolution. This construction does not require a monokinetic cold-plasma ansatz.','B',r'The exact geometric subject is \citet[abstract and pp.~394--406]{marsdenweinstein1982hamiltonian}; the article appeared in 1982 after an August 1981 submission. The opened primary repository abstract explicitly identifies coadjoint orbits of the canonical-transformation group.',['marsdenweinstein1982hamiltonian'],match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='kinetic theory and symplectic geometry')
item(524,'P000973','construction','Gauge-coupled kinetic systems',r'Adding local degrees of freedom and a gauge group is said to lead to further equations, including Yang--Mills.',r'The identifiable extension couples a distribution on particle phase space with internal gauge charge to a gauge field, using a reduced Hamiltonian bracket and constraints. The note does not specify a gauge group, charge orbit, field variables, or an exact Yang--Mills--Vlasov bracket.','B',r'\citet[abstract]{marsdenweinstein1982hamiltonian} explicitly states applicability to the Yang--Mills--Vlasov system. This verifies the direction of development, not a formula absent from the manuscript.',['marsdenweinstein1982hamiltonian'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='geometric mechanics')
item(524,'P000974','remark','Density and the incomplete compressible-fluid Hamiltonian claim',r'The note says vorticity must be specified before a Hamiltonian formalism exists, then refers to bundles over the group carrying density.',r'Compressible-fluid Hamiltonian descriptions require the advected mass density in addition to momentum; a semidirect-product or equivalent cotangent construction provides this. Fixing vorticity is not a general prerequisite for a Hamiltonian formulation. No particular bracket or bundle is specified here.','F',r'The contextual fluid geometry is \citet{arnold1966geometrie}; the nearby compressibility work is discussed more explicitly in record 527. The incomplete vorticity phrase is not identified with a published theorem.',['arnold1966geometrie'],correct='false as written',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, Hamiltonian descriptions of compressible flow are established, but the notebook’s claimed prerequisite is neither necessary in general nor sufficiently specified for a narrower interpretation.',confidence='medium',area='geometric hydrodynamics')
correction('P000973','invariant metric convention','extend by left translations','right translations for the usual Eulerian material-to-spatial convention; left translations after taking inverse maps','Arnold1966 defines the fluid metric with the appropriate right invariance; the convention matters to the Euler equation.','both','ambiguity')

item(525,'P000974','definition','Elastic collisions and the homogeneous Boltzmann equation',r'The collision integral is quadratic in \(f\), with \(v^{\prime},w^{\prime}=(v+w\pm|v-w|n)/2\), \(|n|=1\), and kernel \(g=|v-w|\,d\sigma/d\Omega\).',r'For equal-mass elastic collisions in three dimensions, the displayed postcollision velocities preserve total momentum and kinetic energy. The spatially homogeneous Boltzmann equation is \(\partial_tf=Q(f,f)\), where the angular and relative-speed kernel is nonnegative and the integral is interpreted under the integrability or cancellation hypotheses appropriate to it. Molecular chaos is the modelling assumption behind the product of one-particle densities.','E',r'\citet[§1]{bobylev1984exact} gives the collision equation and conventions. This is standard background, not a theorem identifying a deterministic hard-sphere limit for arbitrary times.',['bobylev1984exact'],match='exact',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000974','claim','Inverse-power potentials and Maxwell molecules',r'For \(V(r)=\alpha r^{-s}\), the collision kernel is \(\widetilde g(\mu)|v-w|^{1-4/s}\). The case \(s=4\) depends only on the angle.',r'For repulsive inverse-power pair potentials in the usual three-dimensional classical scattering convention, homogeneity yields the displayed relative-speed exponent. The inverse-fourth-power potential is the Maxwell-molecule case. This speed independence does not imply that the angular kernel is integrable; an angular cutoff is a separate assumption.','E',r'\citet[§1]{bobylev1984exact} distinguishes true Maxwell molecules from their cutoff or pseudo-Maxwell models. The distinction matters to the later compactness and normalization statements.',['bobylev1984exact'],match='exact',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000974-P000975','method','The Bobylev Fourier identity',r'When the kernel depends only on angle, Fourier transformation greatly simplifies the equation and gives exact solutions.',r'For a homogeneous Maxwell model with integrable angular kernel \(b\), let \(\phi(k)=\int e^{-ik\cdot v}f(v)\,dv\). Then \(\partial_t\phi(k)=\int_{S^2}b(\widehat k\cdot\sigma)[\phi(k^+)\phi(k^-)-\phi(k)\phi(0)]\,d\sigma\), where \(k^\pm=(k\pm|k|\sigma)/2\). Noncutoff versions require the corresponding cancellation interpretation.','E',r'Bobylev’s Fourier method and exact-solution papers appeared in 1975, before this seminar. \citet[§1 and references~4--8]{bobylev1984exact} reproduces the identity and its chronology.',['bobylev1984exact'],match='exact',reconstruction='The Fourier variables and angular-cutoff hypothesis make the shorthand precise.',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000975','theorem','Gaussian convolution symmetry',r'Convolving with a Gaussian density does not change the equation.',r'If \(f(t)\) solves the homogeneous Maxwell-molecule equation and \(G_a\) is a fixed centred normalized isotropic Gaussian, then \(G_a*f(t)\) solves the same equation, whenever both are in the relevant solution class. In Fourier variables this follows from \(|k^+|^2+|k^-|^2=|k|^2\). The variance is constant in time.','E',r'This symmetry belongs to Bobylev’s Maxwell Fourier method \citep[§1]{bobylev1984exact}. The statement is not a symmetry of an arbitrary velocity-dependent collision kernel or of an arbitrary time-dependent convolution.',['bobylev1984exact'],match='exact',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000975','construction','Maxwellians and linearization',r'The stationary family is \(f_M=Ae^{-\alpha|v-v_0|^2}\), called three-parameter for the blocks \(A,\alpha,v_0\). Substitution \(f=f_M(1+F)\) gives the displayed linear collision operator.',r'For \(A,\alpha>0\) and \(v_0\in\mathbb R^3\), these equilibria have five scalar parameters. Linearizing gives \(\mathcal LF=f_M^{-1}[Q(f_MF,f_M)+Q(f_M,f_MF)]\). With detailed balance and the appropriate domain, \(\mathcal L\) is nonpositive and self-adjoint in \(L^2(f_M\,dv)\); its nullspace is spanned by \(1,v_1,v_2,v_3,|v|^2\) for a nondegenerate collision kernel.','E',r'\citet[§§1,3]{bobylev1984exact} treats the Maxwellian and its spectral linearization. The notebook’s integral has specialized the equilibrium scale, mean, and normalization.',['bobylev1984exact'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='The three named blocks contain five real scalar parameters; this repairs a counting ambiguity rather than introducing a new equilibrium family.',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000975','claim','The constant-loss plus compact-gain decomposition',r'The linearized operator is \(CI+T\), with compact \(T\) and \(C=-1\).',r'For an angular-cutoff Maxwell model in the Maxwellian weighted Hilbert space, under the standard integrability assumptions giving compact gain, the linearized collision operator has the form \(-\nu I+K\), with \(K\) compact and constant collision frequency \(\nu>0\). Rescaling time so that \(\nu=1\) yields the notebook formula. Without cutoff the operator is generally unbounded, and for other potentials the loss frequency depends on velocity.','E',r'The spectral setting is discussed in \citet[§3]{bobylev1984exact}. The phrase \(C=-1\) is a normalization, not an intrinsic constant of every inverse-fourth-power gas.',['bobylev1984exact'],match='partial',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000975','claim','Unspecified new eigenfunctions',r'The notes say new eigenfunctions of the linearized equation were obtained, without listing them.',r'No eigenvalue, angular sector, orthogonality convention, or new eigenfunction is given, so this novelty claim cannot be matched to a particular formula independently of the general Maxwell spectral discussion.','F',r'\citet[§3]{bobylev1984exact} supplies the detailed spectral framework, but thematic overlap does not identify which functions this sentence calls new.',['bobylev1984exact'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, the general spectral theory is established; the particular novelty claimed by this sentence remains unidentified.',confidence='medium',area='kinetic theory')
item(525,'P000975','theorem','Triangular linearization of the isotropic Maxwell hierarchy',r'For isotropic \(f(|v|^2)\) and \(s=4\), an infinite system of ordinary differential equations can be transformed into a linear system. The surrounding text mentions general solutions among rapidly decaying functions and says other cases are unknown.',r'In the normalized isotropic cutoff Maxwell model, the Fourier or moment coefficients satisfy a triangular nonlinear hierarchy. On Bobylev’s specified analytic coefficient class, a triangular polynomial change of coefficients conjugates it to \(\dot y_n=-\lambda_n y_n\), \(n\ge2\), with \(\lambda_n=\int_0^1p(s)[1-s^n-(1-s)^n]\,ds\) and nonnegative integrable angular weight \(p\). The analytic class and invertibility estimates are essential; the result is not a blanket linearization for every Schwartz density or every anisotropic model.','A',r'\citet{bobylev1981poincare} was received 4 August 1980 and appeared in 1981. A full explicit proof is in \citet[§4, theorem and Eqs.~(4.8)--(4.14)]{bobylev1984exact}, received May 1984. Its nonresonance and function-space restrictions qualify the notebook’s broad wording.',['bobylev1981poincare','bobylev1984exact'],match='exact',reconstruction='The result is stated for the analytic class used by the primary proof; a larger unrestricted rapidly decaying class is not asserted.',status='As of November 2026, this specified isotropic linearization is established. The manuscript’s phrase unknown in other cases is historical and too unspecific to define an additional open problem.',area='kinetic theory and dynamical systems')
item(525,'P000975','theorem','Countably many nonlinear invariants of relaxation',r'The notes claim infinitely many conservation laws and exact solutions.',r'For non-equilibrium solutions in the preceding normal-coordinate class, fix an index \(m\) with \(y_m\ne0\). Quantities \(|y_n|^{1/\lambda_n}/|y_m|^{1/\lambda_m}\) are constant wherever defined. Finite sets of nonzero normal coordinates also generate explicit special-solution families. These nonlinear functionals differ from the five linear collision invariants expressing mass, momentum, and energy.','A',r'\citet[§4, Corollaries~1--2, Eqs.~(4.15)--(4.21)]{bobylev1984exact} gives these consequences of the hierarchy already announced in \citet{bobylev1981poincare}.',['bobylev1981poincare','bobylev1984exact'],match='exact',area='kinetic theory and dynamical systems')
item(525,'P000975','remark','The spatially inhomogeneous and hydrodynamic-limit programme',r'The spatially inhomogeneous equation is important, and a limiting mean-free-path regime gives Navier--Stokes.',r'The schematic kinetic-to-fluid statement requires a specified Knudsen scaling, collision model, boundary conditions, preparation of initial data, and solution class. Depending on the scaling, limiting equations can instead be compressible or incompressible Euler, acoustic, or diffusive systems. The notebook gives none of these choices.','F',r'\citet[§5]{bobylev1984exact} discusses the limitations of formal hydrodynamic expansions. It does not identify a single rigorous limit theorem with the brief seminar sentence.',['bobylev1984exact'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, many specific hydrodynamic limits are established; no definite unresolved limit problem is reconstructed from this unscaled statement.',area='kinetic theory')
correction('P000975','Maxwellian parameter count','three-parameter family','three parameter blocks, comprising five real scalar parameters in three dimensions','A and alpha are scalar and v0 has three components; the scan confirms the shorthand count.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000975','linearized collision operator','L=-I+compact operator without qualifications','angular-cutoff Maxwell model, suitable weighted space, and collision-frequency normalization nu=1','Without angular cutoff the loss term is not a bounded constant multiple of identity; the collision model and space are omitted.')

item(526,'P000975','problem','The Hermitian sum spectral problem',r'Three decreasing lists \(x,y,z\) satisfy \(\sum_i(x_i+y_i-z_i)=0\). The problem is to characterize when they are spectra of Hermitian \(X,Y,Z\) with \(X+Y=Z\).',r'For real weakly decreasing \(n\)-tuples \(x,y,z\), characterize the existence of complex Hermitian \(n\times n\) matrices \(X,Y\) whose eigenvalue lists are \(x,y\) and whose sum has eigenvalue list \(z\). Trace equality is necessary and is retained in the final characterization.','B',r'Lidskii announced the recursive answer in \citet{lidskii1982spectral}, received October 1981 and published April 1982. A complete resolution follows from the work connected by \citet[§1]{knutsontao1999honeycomb}, first circulated July 1998 and published in 1999.',['lidskii1982spectral','knutsontao1999honeycomb'],correct='correct',match='exact',problem='GP-1980-42',status='As of November 2026, the Horn problem is solved by the recursive inequalities, equivalently by the honeycomb and representation-theoretic criteria.',area='matrix theory and representation theory')
item(526,'P000976','theorem','The recursive Horn inequalities',r'Triples of increasing index lists are declared consistent by a trace-of-indices equality and inequalities for smaller consistent triples; the final answer is \(\sum z_{c_j}\le\sum x_{a_j}+\sum y_{b_j}\).',r'For \(1\le r\le n\), let \(U_r^n\) consist of triples \(I,J,K\) of \(r\)-subsets of \(\{1,\ldots,n\}\) with \(\sum I+\sum J-\sum K=r(r+1)/2\). Set \(T_1^n=U_1^n\); for \(r>1\), retain \((I,J,K)\in U_r^n\) exactly when every \(p<r\) and \((F,G,H)\in T_p^r\) satisfy \(\sum_{f\in F}i_f+\sum_{g\in G}j_g\le\sum_{h\in H}k_h+p(p+1)/2\). Hermitian realizability is equivalent to trace equality and \(\sum_{k\in K}z_k\le\sum_{i\in I}x_i+\sum_{j\in J}y_j\) for all \(r<n\) and \((I,J,K)\in T_r^n\).','B',r'\citet{lidskii1982spectral} is the exact short announcement. \citet{dayso1998spectrum} documents the absence of a published supporting proof at that stage; \citet[§1 and the saturation theorem]{knutsontao1999honeycomb} gives the later route completing Horn’s conjecture.',['lidskii1982spectral','dayso1998spectrum','knutsontao1999honeycomb'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',problem='GP-1980-42',reconstruction='The recursion requires p<r (the notebook writes only ell<n), and the final answer also requires the trace equality already stated on the preceding page.',area='matrix theory')
item(526,'P000976','theorem','Tensor-product multiplicities and saturation',r'The substitution \(a_i\mapsto a_i-i\) simplifies the conditions, and a dual theorem determines which representations occur in a tensor product.',r'An increasing subset \(I=\{i_1<\cdots<i_r\}\) corresponds to the partition \((i_r-r,\ldots,i_1-1)\). For dominant integral \(GL_n\) weights \(\lambda,\mu,\nu\), the Hermitian eigenvalue inequalities with the trace condition characterize positivity of the Littlewood--Richardson multiplicity \(c_{\lambda\mu}^{\nu}\). Equivalence at integral scale uses saturation: \(c_{N\lambda,N\mu}^{N\nu}>0\) for some positive integer \(N\) implies \(c_{\lambda\mu}^{\nu}>0\).','B',r'\citet[Introduction and saturation theorem]{knutsontao1999honeycomb}, preprint July 1998, proves the saturation step and connects it with Klyachko’s 1990s work. The notebook anticipates the relation but contains no proof of this full equivalence.',['knutsontao1999honeycomb'],match='exact',problem='GP-1980-42',area='representation theory')
item(526,'P000976','construction','Convolution of orbital measures',r'The notes add that probability can also be written as a convolution.',r'For fixed spectra \(x,y\), take independent Haar-distributed unitary conjugates of their diagonal matrices. The law of the matrix sum is the convolution of the corresponding orbital probability measures on Hermitian matrices; its spectral pushforward is supported on the Horn polytope. The source gives no density or probability formula beyond this construction.','E',r'The spectral-sum context is \citet{dayso1998spectrum}; identifying a convolution is elementary once the orbital measures are defined. No explicit multiplicity or density algorithm is attributed to the one-line remark.',['dayso1998spectrum'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',confidence='medium',area='probability and matrix theory')
problems.append(dict(id='GP-1980-42',title='Spectra of sums of Hermitian matrices and tensor-product multiplicities',statement=items[-4]['statement'],occurrences=['1980-526.1','1980-526.2','1980-526.3'],status=items[-4]['present_status'],status_date='2026-11',refs=['lidskii1982spectral','knutsontao1999honeycomb']))
correction('P000976','recursive consistency condition','ell<n below the smaller lists','k<ell for the smaller-list recursion (and ell<n for the final spectral inequalities)','The scanned inequality ell<n does not make the definition inductive; the smaller triples belong to T_k^ell.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000976','final answer','ordering plus subset inequalities','ordering, subset inequalities, and the trace equality (1) on P000975','Trace is not repeated in the last formula but is indispensable; the source states it on the preceding page.','both','ambiguity')
