item(527,'P000977','definition','Barotropic compressible Euler equations',r'The notes give \(u_t+(u\cdot\nabla)u=-\rho^{-1}\nabla p\), \(\rho_t+\operatorname{div}(\rho u)=0\), \(p=p(\rho)\), exemplified by \(p=A\rho^\gamma\), and separately say \(T\) is constant.',r'For smooth positive density and a smooth barotropic pressure law, these are the compressible inviscid mass and momentum equations. Strict hyperbolicity of the acoustic part requires \(p^{\prime}(\rho)>0\). Barotropy does not require constant temperature: the polytropic ideal-gas law generally models an isentropic, nonisothermal regime, while the isothermal ideal-gas case has \(\gamma=1\).','E',r'This is the continuum model underlying \citet{ebin1977slightly}. Gelfand’s heating objection identifies a limitation of the simplified thermodynamic model, not a mathematical contradiction in barotropic Euler theory.',['ebin1977slightly'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='Positivity, smoothness, hyperbolicity, and the separation of barotropy from isothermality are supplied.',area='fluid mechanics')
item(527,'P000977','method','Characteristics and the onset of a scalar shock',r'For \(u_t+cu_x=0\), \(u=f(x-ct)\); for \(u_t+uu_x=0\), \(u=f(x-ut)\). The drawing shows a falling flank overturning, and says shocks necessarily occur.',r'For scalar Burgers data \(u(0,x)=f(x)\in C^1\) with bounded derivative, the classical solution is parametrized by \(x=\xi+tf(\xi)\), \(u=f(\xi)\), while \(1+tf^{\prime}(\xi)>0\). If \(\inf f^{\prime}<0\), its first gradient catastrophe is at \(-1/\inf f^{\prime}\); nondecreasing data do not form this forward-time compression shock. Constant data also remain smooth.','E',r'The characteristic calculation is classical background to the entropy theory of \citet{kruzhkov1970first}, received April 1969 and published 1970. The unconditional assertion that nonlinear hyperbolic equations always require shocks is false.',['kruzhkov1970first'],correct='false as written',match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='hyperbolic partial differential equations')
item(527,'P000977-P000978','method','Conservation form and entropy selection',r'After characteristic crossing one should write \(u_t+\frac12(u^2)_x=0\) and define discontinuous solutions.',r'Bounded weak solutions satisfy the distributional conservation law, but that condition alone does not select a unique evolution. The scalar entropy solution obeys the Kruzhkov inequalities; a shock between constant states has Rankine--Hugoniot speed \((u_-+u_+)/2\), and for convex Burgers flux a decreasing jump is admissible.','E',r'\citet{ k ruzhkov1970first} proves existence, uniqueness, and stability in the bounded entropy class. The source’s request to understand discontinuities is thus an exposition of an established theory, not an unresolved uniqueness question.',['kruzhkov1970first'],match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',area='hyperbolic partial differential equations')
item(527,'P000978','claim','Zakharov’s matrix Hopf equation',r'Zakharov says \(U_t+UU_x=0\), with matrix \(U\), can also be integrated completely.',r'For matrix-valued \(U\), multiplication order matters: \(UU_x\) is generally not \(\frac12(U^2)_x\). The source provides no spectral assumptions, initial-data class, solution formula, or definition of complete integration. Diagonal or mutually commuting reductions reduce to scalar characteristic equations, but that is only a restricted case.','F',r'The nearby scalar theory does not certify the unqualified matrix claim. Searches for matrix Hopf and Zakharov noncommutative hydrodynamic systems did not identify a primary theorem with exactly these unspecified hypotheses. No general integrability theorem is inferred.',['kruzhkov1970first'],correct='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',speaker='V. E. Zakharov',status='As of November 2026, the exact general matrix-integration assertion in this note remains unidentified; scalar commuting reductions are elementary and do not settle it.',confidence='medium',area='integrable systems')
item(527,'P000978','question','Expansion of gas into vacuum',r'For a vessel initially divided into gas and vacuum, removing the partition is said to produce a system of shocks moving back and forth.',r'The initial expansion of a positive-pressure gas into vacuum in the standard one-dimensional isentropic Riemann problem is described by a rarefaction toward vacuum. Later wall reflections depend on the equation of state, boundary conditions, and geometry. The diagram alone does not determine a universal shock-train evolution or equilibration theorem.','F',r'The displayed compressible equations permit both compression and expansion waves; the scalar entropy context is \citet{kruzhkov1970first}. No exact vessel problem or solution theorem was found that warrants the notebook’s unqualified description.',['kruzhkov1970first'],correct='false as written',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',status='As of November 2026, the recorded vessel question is underspecified; its blanket initial shock description is not valid for expansion into vacuum.',confidence='medium',area='fluid mechanics')
item(527,'P000978','claim','The hyperbolic-plane nonimmersion analogy',r'The notes connect the preceding shock discussion to the assertion that the Lobachevsky plane does not embed in \(\mathbb R^3\).',r'The classical geometric obstruction concerns a complete regular isometric immersion of the constant-negative-curvature hyperbolic plane into Euclidean three-space, with the smoothness required by Hilbert’s theorem. Topological or smooth embeddings without preservation of the metric exist. The notebook supplies no argument connecting this theorem to the gas example.','E',r'The intended geometric result is \citet{hilbert1901flachen}; the primary AMS access attempt was blocked, so the bibliography records that limitation. The isometric and completeness qualifications are indispensable and the asserted causal link to shocks is not established.',['hilbert1901flachen'],correct='false as written',match='partial',verification='indirect',status='As of November 2026, the qualified Hilbert nonimmersion theorem is classical; the unqualified embedding sentence and the suggested gas analogy are not valid as a proof.',confidence='medium',area='differential geometry')
item(527,'P000978','theorem','The slightly compressible to incompressible limit',r'Krein says that with density close to one, a parameter limit gives incompressible-fluid solutions.',r'For the smooth slightly compressible models and compatible, suitably prepared initial data of Ebin’s theorem, solutions approach the incompressible motion on a common time interval where the latter remains smooth. A small Mach parameter, appropriate control of acoustic oscillations, and boundary compatibility are essential; arbitrary data and passage through shock formation are not covered.','E',r'The identifiable result is \citet{ebin1977slightly}, published in January 1977, before this lecture. The opened Annals record verifies the source and its strong-constraint interpretation; the notebook does not state the exact function spaces or convergence norm.',['ebin1977slightly'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='This is a calibrated description of the source theorem, not a new quantified convergence statement without its original hypotheses.',confidence='medium',area='fluid mechanics')
item(527,'P000978','construction','Internal energy in material coordinates',r'For the Jacobian \(g(x)\) of the flow map, the potential energy is \(\int_\Omega\int_1^{g^{-1}(x)}p(\rho)\rho^{-2}\,d\rho\,dx\).',r'With unit reference mass density, write the material flow as \(\eta(X)\), its positive Jacobian as \(J(X)\), and \(\rho(\eta(X))=J(X)^{-1}\). If specific internal energy obeys \(e^{\prime}(\rho)=p(\rho)/\rho^2\), then \(\int e(J^{-1})\,dX\) is the potential energy up to an additive constant. In Eulerian coordinates the same energy is \(\int \rho e(\rho)\,dx\).','E',r'This is the mechanical energy interpretation used in the strongly constrained flow setting of \citet{ebin1977slightly}. The formula is consistent once the integral is read in material coordinates.',['ebin1977slightly'],match='partial',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='The reference measure and distinction between specific energy and energy density are supplied explicitly.',area='geometric hydrodynamics')
item(527,'P000978','remark','The unspecific viscous-fluid extension',r'The notes say the same exists for a viscous fluid, following Arnold.',r'Viscosity adds a dissipative stress and destroys conservation of the inviscid Hamiltonian in general. A geometric formulation can still be given, but the sentence does not specify whether it refers to a manifold description, a limit theorem, or a Hamiltonian structure with extra variables.','F',r'\citet{arnold1966geometrie} supplies the ideal-fluid geometric context; it does not make a viscous equation an energy-conserving geodesic flow by this sentence alone.',['arnold1966geometrie'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',status='As of November 2026, the intended viscous analogue remains unspecified in this record.',confidence='medium',area='fluid mechanics')
item(527,'P000978','problem','A descending step for a polynomially regularized Burgers equation',r'Gelfand asks what happens to a descending step profile for \(u_t+uu_x+p(\partial_x)u=0\), with \(p\) a polynomial. The final sentence says KdV continually sheds solitons.',r'Long-time behaviour depends on the polynomial and on the solution concept: \(p=0\) gives entropy shocks, \(p(D)=-\nu D^2\), \(\nu>0\), gives viscous smoothing and travelling shocks, and a real cubic dispersive term gives the KdV dispersive-shock problem. Some polynomial symbols cause linear ill-posedness. The notes prescribe neither coefficients nor admissible class, so there is no single well-posed universal evolution question.','F',r'For the KdV subcase, \citet{gurevichpitaevskii1974shock}, submitted April 1973, Russian publication 1973 and English 1974, describes the expanding modulated oscillatory zone with a soliton edge. This is a concrete prior result, not an answer for every polynomial.',['gurevichpitaevskii1974shock'],correct='not determinable',match='partial',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',status='As of November 2026, several specified subcases are established; the unrestricted polynomial question remains an underspecified programme, not a newly indexed open conjecture.',confidence='high',area='dispersive partial differential equations')
correction('P000977','barotropic assumption','T is constant','Barotropy p=p(rho) does not by itself impose constant temperature','The separate line is literal; polytropic isentropic and isothermal laws are different closures.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000977-P000978','shock inevitability','shock waves necessarily arise / discontinuities always needed','compression can produce shocks; constant and suitable expanding smooth data do not','The characteristic map x=xi+t f(xi) fails only when 1+t fprime(xi)=0.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000978','Lobachevsky-plane assertion','does not embed in R3','has no complete regular isometric immersion in Euclidean R3 under Hilbert’s smoothness hypotheses','Without isometry, the hyperbolic plane is diffeomorphic to a disc and embeds.','both','faithful_error')

item(528,'P000978','construction','The semigroup of indefinite contractions',r'For \(J=\operatorname{diag}(-I_p,I_q)\), a matrix \(A\) is a J-contraction when \(\langle Ax,Ax\rangle_J\le\langle x,x\rangle_J\). Such matrices form a semigroup.',r'The inequality is \(A^*JA\le J\). If both \(A,B\) satisfy it, then \((AB)^*J(AB)=B^*A^*JAB\le B^*JB\le J\). The full matrix semigroup can include singular matrices; its invertible part is the part naturally compared with semigroups inside a complex Lie group.','E',r'This is an elementary model for the constructions in \citet[§§3,6]{olshanskii1981cones}, received December 1980 and published in 1981. The singular boundary must not be conflated with the group-contained semigroup \(G\exp(iC)\).',['olshanskii1981cones'],correct='correct',match='exact',area='Lie semigroups')
item(528,'P000978-P000979','construction','Compression semigroups of domains',r'Examples are hyperbolic motions taking a half-plane into itself, and transformations of the compact dual of a bounded symmetric domain \(D\) taking \(D\) into itself. The matrix ball gives J-contractions.',r'For a fixed domain \(D\) in a homogeneous space and an ambient transformation group, the set \(\{g:gD\subseteq D\}\) is a semigroup. For a Hermitian symmetric domain embedded in its compact dual, the connected complex group supplies the compression semigroup identified with the maximal-cone Olshanskii semigroup in the cited theorem. Matrix-ball realizations connect this geometry with appropriately normalized indefinite contractions.','A',r'\citet[§6, Thm.~6.2]{olshanskii1981cones} proves the symmetric-domain identification. The elementary closure property applies also to the real half-plane example, but does not classify arbitrary domain-preserving maps.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',reconstruction='The ambient group, connected component, and distinction from all holomorphic self-maps are made explicit.',area='Lie semigroups and complex geometry')
item(528,'P000979','definition','Contractive involutive-semigroup representations',r'The notes require \(T(ab)=T(a)T(b)\), \(T(a^\#)=T(a)^*\), and \(\|T(a)\|\le1\), where \((ab)^\#=b^\#a^\#\).',r'A representation in the category used here is a continuous nondegenerate homomorphism of a topological involutive semigroup into bounded Hilbert-space contractions, respecting the involution. For a unital semigroup one takes \(T(1)=I\); weak operator continuity is sufficient in the primary definition and yields strong continuity in the setting considered.','A',r'The exact definition appears in \citet[§4.2]{olshanskii1981cones}. Continuity and nondegeneracy are necessary category qualifications absent from the abbreviated notebook list.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',area='representation theory')
item(528,'P000979','construction','Tensor-power representations of ordinary contractions',r'For contractions on \(V=\mathbb C^n\), tensor powers give analytic representations indexed by sequences; anti-analytic representations also occur.',r'The tensor-power action \(A\mapsto A^{\otimes m}\) restricts to the Schur modules \(S_\lambda V\), indexed by partitions \(\lambda\vdash m\) with at most \(n\) rows, and remains contractive. Conjugating gives antiholomorphic examples. These are polynomial holomorphic representations; the notebook does not justify classifying every continuous involutive-semigroup representation by these partitions alone.','E',r'The examples motivate the analytic category in \citet[Introduction and §4]{olshanskii1981cones}. Ordinary matrix-contraction representations and the later \(\Gamma(C_{\min})\) theorem must be kept distinct.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='partial',reconstruction='The sequences are interpreted as partitions for polynomial representations, without extending the claim to an unrestricted continuous category.',area='representation theory')
item(528,'P000979-P000980','theorem','The Hermitian criterion for invariant proper cones',r'A convex adjoint-invariant cone with nonempty interior exists in a simple real Lie algebra exactly when it is of Hermitian type.',r'For a noncompact simple real Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\), the existence of a nonzero closed convex pointed generating adjoint-invariant cone is equivalent to \(\mathfrak g\) being of Hermitian type. Nonempty interior alone is insufficient: \(\mathfrak g\) itself is an invariant convex cone. For \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak k\oplus\mathfrak p\), the Hermitian condition is \(\dim Z(\mathfrak k)=1\).','E',r'\citet[Thm.~1.2]{olshanskii1981cones} states the Vinberg--Kostant criterion and specifies the nondegenerate cone convention. This is explicitly attributed earlier work, even though its use in the speaker’s paper is new.',['olshanskii1981cones'],correct='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='Closedness, pointedness, generating property, and noncompactness are supplied.',area='Lie theory')
item(528,'P000979','theorem','The list of simple Hermitian real forms',r'The list contains \(\mathfrak{su}(p,q)\), \(\mathfrak{sp}(2n,\mathbb R)\), \(\mathfrak{so}^*(2n)\), \(\mathfrak{so}(2,n)\), and two exceptional forms.',r'The noncompact simple Hermitian families are these classical real forms, with parameter ranges making the Lie algebra simple and noncompact and with low-rank coincidences identified, together with \(\mathfrak e_{6(-14)}\) and \(\mathfrak e_{7(-25)}\). For example \(\mathfrak{so}(2,2)\) is semisimple rather than simple and must be excluded from the simple list.','E',r'The criterion and root-theoretic conventions are in \citet[§1]{olshanskii1981cones}; the exceptional names expand the source’s shorthand.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',reconstruction='Exceptional signatures and the low-rank simplicity qualification are supplied.',area='Lie theory')
item(528,'P000979-P000980','theorem','Minimal and maximal cones after choosing an orientation',r'There are unique \(C_{\min}\subset C_{\max}\), and a Cartan-subalgebra description, with attribution to Vinberg, Olshanskii, and Paneitz.',r'After choosing a positive orientation in the one-dimensional centre of the maximal compact algebra, the positive invariant proper cones have a unique minimal and maximal member. Their negatives give the opposite orientation, so uniqueness without a sign choice is false. Intersection with a compact Cartan subspace identifies the intermediate cones with Weyl-invariant cones between the corresponding root-theoretic extrema.','A',r'\citet[Thms.~1.5,1.7,1.9 and Cor.~1.6]{olshanskii1981cones} gives the precise bounds and classification, distinguishing Vinberg’s earlier maximal cone from the new Cartan-cone description. The paper was received 4 December 1980.',['olshanskii1981cones'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='A positive central direction, and hence an orientation of the cone, is fixed before stating uniqueness.',area='Lie theory')
item(528,'P000980','theorem','Global semigroups from Hermitian invariant cones',r'For every cone under discussion, \(\Gamma(C)=G\exp(iC)\) is a semigroup inside \(G_{\mathbb C}\).',r'Let \(\mathfrak g\) be a noncompact simple Hermitian real Lie algebra, \(G_{\mathbb C}\) a connected complex Lie group with Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g_{\mathbb C}\), and \(G\) its connected analytic subgroup with Lie algebra \(\mathfrak g\). For a closed pointed generating adjoint-invariant cone \(C\subset\mathfrak g\), \(G\exp(iC)\) is a closed semigroup, and polar multiplication gives a homeomorphism \(G\times C\to\Gamma(C)\). Appropriate universal covers are used for representations that do not descend to this \(G\).','A',r'This is \citet[Thm.~3.5 and §4.1]{olshanskii1981cones}, after identifying the paper’s cone in \(i\mathfrak g\) with \(iC\). The theorem does not claim this global polar description for arbitrary Lie algebras or arbitrary convex cones.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',reconstruction='The Lie-group, cone, and covering hypotheses are stated explicitly.',area='Lie semigroups')
item(528,'P000980','theorem','Local semigroups for general invariant cones',r'A weaker local version is said to hold for every Lie algebra.',r'If \(M\) is a connected finite-dimensional Lie group and \(C\) a closed pointed generating invariant convex cone in its Lie algebra, the germ at the identity of \(\exp(C\cap U)\), with \(U\) shrinking to zero, is a local semigroup. This is a statement about products sufficiently near the identity, not automatic global closure of an exponential image.','A',r'\citet[Thm.~3.3]{olshanskii1981cones} proves this assertion independently of the Hermitian global theorem.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',area='Lie semigroups')
item(528,'P000980','theorem','The representation cone and highest-weight condition',r'The note defines \(C(T)\) by a nonpositivity condition on infinitesimal generators and says it is nontrivial exactly for highest-weight representations.',r'For a strongly continuous unitary representation \(\pi\), use the self-adjoint closures to define \(C_\pi=\{Y\in\mathfrak g:i\,d\pi(Y)\le0\}\). For a nontrivial irreducible unitary representation of the simply connected Hermitian group, a nonzero such cone, with its orientation fixed, characterizes a unitary highest-weight representation; the opposite orientation interchanges highest and lowest weight. The trivial representation has \(C_\pi=\mathfrak g\) and is a separate exception.','A',r'\citet[Thms.~2.2,2.5]{olshanskii1981cones} gives the exact nontriviality, irreducibility, and sign qualifications. The paper works in \(i\mathfrak g\); the formula here pulls that convention back to \(\mathfrak g\).',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',reconstruction='The infinitesimal generator, operator closure, irreducibility, exclusion of the trivial case, and orientation are supplied.',area='representation theory')
item(528,'P000980','theorem','Holomorphic extension and the contractive representation category',r'A highest-weight representation extends holomorphically to \(\Gamma(C(T))\), called the exact extension domain, and there is a category equivalence.',r'For a fixed positive invariant proper cone \(C\), unitary representations satisfying \(C\subset C_\pi\) extend uniquely to contractive involutive representations on the appropriate covering semigroup, holomorphic on its interior, with \(T(g\exp(iY))=\pi(g)\exp(i,d\pi(Y))\). Restriction is an equivalence of the corresponding categories. The cone \(C_\pi\) is maximal for contractive extension in this setting, not necessarily for all analytic continuations of every individual matrix coefficient.','A',r'\citet[Thms.~4.5--4.6 and Thm.~2.9]{olshanskii1981cones} provides the extension, uniqueness, and direct-integral/category formulation. The source’s phrase all highest-weight representations requires the compatibility condition with the chosen cone.',['olshanskii1981cones'],match='exact',reconstruction='The cone condition and the word contractive make precise both the category and the maximality assertion.',area='representation theory and complex geometry')
item(528,'P000980','theorem','Holomorphic–antiholomorphic factorization for the minimal cone',r'Irreducible representations are said to be tensor products of holomorphic and antiholomorphic irreducible representations.',r'For the involutive semigroup associated with the minimal positive cone, in the continuous contractive category and on the cover specified by the theorem, every irreducible representation is the tensor product of a holomorphic highest-weight extension and the conjugate of another such extension. The theorem as checked does not assert the same unrestricted formulation for every intermediate cone.','A',r'This is the exact scope of \citet[Cor.~5.8]{olshanskii1981cones}, whose notation explicitly has \(C_{\min}\). The notebook has dropped that qualifier.',['olshanskii1981cones'],correct='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',reconstruction='The minimal-cone hypothesis is restored from the primary theorem.',area='representation theory')
item(528,'P000980','remark','Gelfand’s request to go beyond semisimple groups',r'Gelfand objects to restricting the theory to simple or semisimple algebras, especially for infinite-dimensional representation theory.',r'This is a programme to extend invariant-cone and semigroup methods beyond the finite-dimensional semisimple setting. No class of nonsemisimple or infinite-dimensional groups, topology, cone condition, or target theorem is specified.','F',r'\citet[Introduction]{olshanskii1981cones} already discusses an oscillator example as motivation. That overlap is thematic and does not solve an unspecified universal extension problem.',['olshanskii1981cones'],correct='not determinable',match='thematic only',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',status='As of November 2026, this remark remains a broad direction in the record, without a definite question suitable for a new problem-index entry.',area='representation theory')
