byid['1980-519.1']['statement']=r'Here positive part means the differential part of a formal pseudodifferential operator. The commuting pair is \(L_4=L^2+2x\), \(L_6=L^3+\tfrac32(xL+Lx)=L^3+3xL+3D\), with \([L_4,L_6]=0\) and \(L_6^2=L_4^3\). The two-term \(A=L+xL^{-1}\) in the notes instead gives \((A^3)_+=L^3+3xL+6D\), so this formula also requires correction. The actual formal square root of \(L_4\) begins \(L+xL^{-1}-DL^{-2}+\cdots\). Replacing \(L\) by \(D^2+x^3+a\) gives the nonsingular elliptic Dixmier cubic when \(a\ne0\).'
byid['1980-519.1']['fate']=r'The construction is due to \citet{dixmier1968weyl}. Both the displayed sixth-order alternative and the two-term formal-root description require correction; this chapter verifies the emendations directly. The parameter-free source specialization has the singular cubic \(w^2=z^3\), whose normalization has genus zero.'
byid['1980-516.7']['notes']=r'\(S(\alpha)=\sum_{j=0}^3w_j\sigma_j\otimes\sigma_j\), with \(w_0=1\), \(w_1=\operatorname{sn}(x)/\operatorname{sn}(x+\alpha)\), \(w_2=[\operatorname{dn}(x+\alpha)/\operatorname{sn}(x+\alpha)][\operatorname{sn}(x)/\operatorname{dn}(x)]\), and \(w_3=[\operatorname{cn}(x+\alpha)/\operatorname{sn}(x+\alpha)][\operatorname{sn}(x)/\operatorname{cn}(x)]\). The last denominator is overwritten in the autograph; \(\operatorname{cn}\) is the tentative reading.'
