byid['1980-524.3']['statement']=r'On canonical phase space \(T^*\mathbb R^d\), identified with \((x,v)\) in unit-mass coordinates, the Lie--Poisson bracket is \(\{F,G\}(f)=\int f\{\delta F/\delta f,\delta G/\delta f\}_{x,v}\,dx\,dv\), with a consistent overall sign convention. A rigorous algebraic core consists of smooth compactly supported densities and cylindrical polynomial functionals built from pairings with compactly supported smooth test functions. For more general functionals, smooth variational derivatives and decay sufficient for all integrals and integrations by parts are assumed. The kinetic-plus-self-field Hamiltonian formally yields Vlasov evolution within its smooth domain; this construction alone is not an analytic existence theorem and does not require a monokinetic ansatz.'
